## The idea

One CPU, so **whoever is on top of the call stack is running**. Keep the stack and `prev`, the first tick not yet charged to anyone. On a start, charge the current top up to now and push the new function. On an end, charge the top including this tick (+1) and pop.

## Step by step

1. On "start" at t: if the stack is not empty, top += t − prev. Push the id. prev = t.
2. On "end" at t: top += t − prev + 1. Pop. prev = t + 1.

## Worked example

**Input:** n = 2: 0:start:0, 1:start:2, 1:end:5, 0:end:6

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| 0 starts at 0 | stack [0], prev 0 |
| 1 starts at 2 | charge 0 for 2 → [2,0]; stack [0,1]; prev 2 |
| 1 ends at 5 | charge 1 for 5 − 2 + 1 = 4; prev 6 |
| 0 ends at 6 | charge 0 for 6 − 6 + 1 = 1 → 3 |

**Result:** [3, 4]

## Cost

O(number of logs) time, O(depth) memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

